{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.LPDuality","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mathlib.LPDuality","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Group.Finset.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Ring.Finset","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.BigOperators.Group.Finset","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Linarith"],"declarations":4,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.LPDuality.duality_gap_le_of_residual_bounds","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.LPDuality.duality_gap_le_of_residual_bounds","kind":"theorem","line":118,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/LPDuality.lean#L118-L140","doc":"**Certified gap bound.** If every primal slack residual and every dual\nslack residual is at most `ε ≥ 0` in the (signed) sense below, the gap is\nat most `(n + m) · ε`.\n\nThis is the form a numerical consumer can discharge: it bounds the true\ngap by quantities it computes itself from the emitted certificate, with\nno appeal to the solver's own `duality_gap` field. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.LPDuality.duality_gap_decomposition","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.LPDuality.duality_gap_decomposition","kind":"theorem","line":82,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/LPDuality.lean#L82-L116","doc":"**Duality-gap decomposition.** An unconditional identity: the primal-dual\ngap is exactly the sum of the primal complementary-slackness residuals\n`(c_j - (Aᵀy)_j) · x_j` and the dual ones `y_i · ((Ax)_i - b_i)`.\n\nNo feasibility hypothesis is needed — this is algebra, and that is the\npoint. A solver reports a `duality_gap` scalar; this identity lets a\nconsumer *recompute* that gap from the emitted `x`, `y`, `A`, `b`, `c`\nalone, so the reported number becomes checkable rather than trusted.\n`weak_duality` above says the gap is non-negative under feasibility;\nthis says *where it lives*. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.LPDuality.strong_duality","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.LPDuality.strong_duality","kind":"theorem","line":51,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/LPDuality.lean#L51-L79","doc":"**Strong LP duality** (certificate form). Given complementary slackness,\n`∑ c_j x_j = ∑ b_i y_i`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.LPDuality.weak_duality","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.LPDuality.weak_duality","kind":"theorem","line":21,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/LPDuality.lean#L21-L49","doc":"**Weak LP duality.** `∑ b_i y_i ≤ ∑ c_j x_j` for primal-feasible `x`\nand dual-feasible `y`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}